<TopicPage slug="subjects/geography/intermediate/urban-geography-and-cities">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Cities are <strong>humanity's best invention for exchanging ideas — and its most expensive
    mistakes</strong>, sometimes in the same block. Urban geography explains where cities grow,
    why land prices gradient outward from centres, how neighbourhoods sort themselves, and why
    the coming decades (billions of new urban residents) are largely a design question.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four ideas, from von Thünen's rings to Jacobs' streets" widget="equationStep" config={urbanCfg} />

  <h2>The debates running now</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Housing affordability:</strong> supply restriction (zoning, height limits) meets exploding demand in every desirable city. Economists increasingly agree scarcity is central; the politics of building remains the hard part.</li>
    <li><strong>Cars vs people:</strong> mid-century urbanism built for cars (highways through neighbourhoods, parking minimums); the counter-movement — walkability, cycling, transit — measurably cuts deaths and raises street-level commerce (Paris' 15-minute city experiment is the current test case).</li>
    <li><strong>Informal settlements:</strong> a billion people live in self-built districts. Eviction-only policies fail; upgrading (water, power, tenure) shows better results — Dharavi in Mumbai is both a slum and a functioning economic engine.</li>
    <li><strong>Sprawl and climate:</strong> urban form locks in energy use for a century. Dense, transit-served growth is among the highest-leverage climate policies available (the environmental ladder connects).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Glaeser, Triumph of the City; Jacobs, The Death and Life of Great American Cities (1961); UN-Habitat World Cities Report.</p>
</TopicPage>
